# Vethida database backups.
#
# Install:  sudo install -m 755 -o root -g root vethida-backup.sh \
#                        /usr/local/bin/vethida-backup.sh
#           sudo cp pg_dump.cron /etc/cron.d/vethida-backup
#           sudo chown root:root /etc/cron.d/vethida-backup
#           sudo chmod 644 /etc/cron.d/vethida-backup
#
# /etc/cron.d entries need a user field (the 6th column) and a PATH, because cron
# runs with almost none of the environment a login shell has. A crontab that works
# when pasted into `crontab -e` and fails here is usually missing exactly that.
#
# Secrets are NOT in this file. DATABASE_URL, and the R2 variables when an
# offsite copy is wanted, live in /etc/vethida/backup.env, root-owned and chmod
# 600, which vethida-backup.sh sources. A cron file is world-readable by design.
#
# One script in three modes, not three scripts. These lines named
# vethida-backup.sh, vethida-verify-restore.sh and vethida-backup-age-check.sh,
# none of which existed anywhere, so installing this file bought three failures a
# month written to a log nobody opens.

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
MAILTO=""

# Nightly dump at 03:17 UTC.
#
# Not 03:00. Every cron on every box fires on the hour, and a managed database
# doing its own maintenance at the same moment turns a backup into contention.
# An odd minute costs nothing and avoids the crowd.
17 3 * * * vethida /usr/local/bin/vethida-backup.sh dump >> /var/log/vethida/backup.log 2>&1

# Weekly restore verification, Sunday 04:40 UTC.
#
# This is the half people skip. A dump nobody has restored is a file, not a
# backup, and the first restore attempt should not be during an incident. The
# script restores into a scratch database, checks the row counts are plausible,
# and drops it.
40 4 * * 0 vethida /usr/local/bin/vethida-backup.sh verify >> /var/log/vethida/backup.log 2>&1

# Monthly alarm if the newest dump is stale.
#
# Backups fail silently far more often than they fail loudly: a full disk, a
# rotated credential, a renamed database. Without this the first sign is an empty
# backups directory on the day it matters.
0 9 1 * * vethida /usr/local/bin/vethida-backup.sh age-check >> /var/log/vethida/backup.log 2>&1
